"A baby tooth gets loose before it falls out.": "ad1b17b7e226",
"A tooth has layers. The outside is enamel, the hardest substance in your body. Under it is dentin, which is softer and a little yellow, and in the center is the pulp, which has nerves and blood vessels. The roots hold the tooth tightly in the jaw bone. Teeth are hard like bones, but they are not bones: a broken bone can heal, but enamel cannot grow back, so a hole in a tooth stays a hole until a dentist fills it.": "87d00faabff1",
"Baby teeth are not just for practice. They help you chew and speak clearly, and they hold space in your jaw for the adult teeth. The adult teeth have been growing inside your jaw bone for years, right under the baby teeth. As an adult tooth moves up, the roots of the baby tooth above it slowly melt away. That is why a loose baby tooth has almost no root when it finally falls out.": "daecf1701209",
"Brush for two minutes, twice a day, with fluoride toothpaste, and clean between your teeth. In Taiwan, elementary students rinse with a fluoride mouthwash at school once a week, and first and second graders can get sealants: a thin coating that fills the deep grooves of the six-year molars, where food likes to hide. Eat fewer sweets and sugary drinks, and see a dentist every six months. Now count your own teeth with the tooth chart below the model.": "c450fa4f7bf7",
"Cavities start with bacteria that live on your teeth in a sticky film called plaque. When you eat sugar, the bacteria eat it too and make acid, which slowly dissolves the enamel. If the hole reaches the dentin and then the pulp, the tooth can really hurt. Fluoride helps enamel stay strong and can even repair the very earliest damage, which is why most toothpaste has fluoride in it.": "e10013cca63c",
"Chew your food well before you swallow.": "7a2c20e8f34a",
"Different teeth do different jobs. The flat incisors at the front bite and cut, like scissors. The pointed canines next to them grip and tear. The premolars and molars at the back have wide, bumpy tops for grinding food into a mash. Your first adult molars come in around age 6, behind the baby teeth, without pushing any baby tooth out, so many people do not even notice them. They are called six-year molars.": "9e654792bcee",
"Enamel protects the outside of each tooth.": "425faf2680aa",
"How do cavities form?": "fb07305f584d",
"I brush my teeth after breakfast and before bed.": "0c6199e5af16",
"My front tooth is so loose that it wiggles.": "54641f89a9d3",
"My little sister lost her first tooth today.": "ad154203b25e",
"Smile in a mirror and count your teeth. Most children have all 20 of their baby teeth by about age 3. Starting around age 6, the baby teeth fall out one by one, and adult teeth take their place. By about age 12 or 13, most children have lost all of their baby teeth. An adult can have up to 32 teeth, but there is no third set. Adult teeth have to last for the rest of your life.": "7d92d25ad13a",
